{
  "name": "grep",
  "description": "Search your records, explicitly shared team communication accounts, and current agent tree.\n\n- Default scope is `all` (own plus explicit direct team account grants). Use `scope=\"own\"` for personal-only or `scope=\"shared\"` for shared-only; `team_id`, `owner_user_id`, and `account_id` narrow results before counting/paging. Result ownership identifies each owner, account and team. Never infer sharing from a shared project or access another owner's credentials/iPhone.\n- `pattern` is a regex over record text. Omit it for metadata-only agent searches.\n- `sources` selects one or more sources; omitted defaults: messages, network, agent_network. `gmail` aliases email.\n- `shared_project_agents` lists/searches titles, statuses and project names in explicitly shared projects. Omit pattern to list; use filters.project_id and offset/head_limit to page. It never reads goals, trajectories, credentials or environment.\n- `agent_trajectories` contains prior user-owned chat, SMS, and Temporal runs, including R2 archives; `agent_network` contains the current tree.\n- Typed agent parameters: `role` (USER, ASSISTANT, TOOL), `record_kind` (message, llm_output, llm_context, all), `surface`, `trace_path`, `since`, `until`. Agent parameters without `sources` select agent_trajectories.\n- `trace_path` at a root or `/root/...` path spans its subtree; a child id narrows to one agent. For trajectory pages, keep the same query and set `page_after` to `next_page_after`; `order` selects newest_first or oldest_first.\n- Other source-specific narrowing goes in `filters`, using the keys its schema lists per source. A flat key applies to every selected source that accepts it; `{\"<source>\": {...}}` targets one source.\n- `calendar` and `drive` search Google Calendar and Drive. Paths are folders and files; omit `pattern` to list or read one.\n- Flags: -A after, -B before, -C both, -n line numbers, -i case insensitive, head_limit, offset, multiline.\n- Results include trace_path, message_role, agent_title, timestamps, and nearby text.\n- Use web_search for the public web. A \"source not connected\" notice means that connector is unavailable. For documents, require_source_access applies only when the user names the provider.",
  "parameters": {
    "type": "object",
    "properties": {
      "pattern": {
        "anyOf": [
          {
            "type": "string"
          },
          {
            "type": "null"
          }
        ],
        "default": null,
        "description": "Regular expression over record text. Omit for agent_trajectories or agent_network or shared_project_agents to list recent records using metadata filters. Omit for calendar or drive to list a day or folder, or to read the one event or file a path names.",
        "title": "Pattern"
      },
      "sources": {
        "anyOf": [
          {
            "items": {
              "enum": [
                "messages",
                "email",
                "gmail",
                "imessage",
                "whatsapp",
                "network",
                "shared_project_agents",
                "agent_network",
                "agent_trajectories",
                "transactions",
                "documents",
                "calendar",
                "drive",
                "summary"
              ],
              "type": "string"
            },
            "type": "array"
          },
          {
            "type": "null"
          }
        ],
        "default": null,
        "description": "The specific data source(s) to search - pass a list and pick the exact source(s) the task needs. Do NOT default to a blunt catch-all when you know the channel; name the concrete sources (e.g. [\"email\"], or [\"email\", \"imessage\"]). Omit entirely to search the local defaults (messages, network, agent_network). Channels of the user's communications: email searches the user's email/Gmail; imessage searches iMessage (including SMS stored in the Mac Messages DB); whatsapp searches WhatsApp. messages is the umbrella over all communication channels (use only when the channel is genuinely unknown). network searches contact records. agent_network searches all message roles in the current agent tree and returns role/location metadata. agent_trajectories searches prior user-owned agent runs across chat, SMS, and Temporal; it excludes the current agent tree. Results carry a trace path, message role, and agent title as separate fields. Trajectory record_kind=message is a stored agent message; llm_output is a model response; llm_context is text passed to a model and may replay earlier turns. Ordinary results include messages and outputs; record_kind=all includes replayed inputs. transactions searches synced Plaid transaction rows. documents searches synced ChatGPT/Claude exports, Apple Notes, Obsidian vault notes, Granola meeting notes, and generic user documents. calendar searches the user's Google Calendar events; drive searches Google Drive file names, folders, and file text. summary returns a compact, precomputed inventory with message counts, date ranges, channel totals, and threads ranked by volume. Use sources=[\"summary\"] first for aggregate questions such as total messages, date coverage, or most-contacted threads; then use the concrete channel source to inspect exact message text. Thread identifiers in the summary are valid contact identifiers when no human-readable name is stored. If a named source's connector isn't connected, the search returns a 'source not connected' notice - do NOT retry that source; continue with the sources you can reach.",
        "title": "Sources"
      },
      "source": {
        "anyOf": [
          {
            "enum": [
              "messages",
              "email",
              "gmail",
              "imessage",
              "whatsapp",
              "network",
              "shared_project_agents",
              "agent_network",
              "agent_trajectories",
              "transactions",
              "documents",
              "calendar",
              "drive",
              "summary"
            ],
            "type": "string"
          },
          {
            "type": "null"
          }
        ],
        "default": null,
        "description": "Legacy single-source alias for `sources`. Prefer `sources` (a list of the exact sources the task needs).",
        "title": "Source"
      },
      "scope": {
        "anyOf": [
          {
            "enum": [
              "own",
              "shared",
              "all"
            ],
            "type": "string"
          },
          {
            "type": "null"
          }
        ],
        "default": null,
        "description": "Default all: your own records plus explicitly shared team accounts. own limits to your data; shared requires direct accepted team account grants. No transitive access.",
        "title": "Scope"
      },
      "team_id": {
